QB.add(48,[{"n":1,"q":"Which of the following statements regarding von Willebrand disease is incorrect?","o":[{"t":"Type 2 von Willebrand disease is associated with a moderate bleeding tendency"},{"t":"Type 3 von Willebrand disease is associated with a complete absence of factor VIII"},{"t":"Type 1 von Willebrand disease presents with severe bleeding since childhood"},{"t":"Type 2 von Willebrand disease includes subtypes with varying defects in von Willebrand factor."}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Type 1 von Willebrand disease presents with severe bleeding since childhood:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This statement is incorrect. Type 1 von Willebrand disease is the mildest form of the condition and is characterized by a partial deficiency of von Willebrand factor (vWF). Patients typically experience mild bleeding symptoms rather than severe ones. It often presents with mild bleeding tendencies rather than severe, and symptoms may not be apparent until later in life."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Type 2 von Willebrand disease is associated with a moderate bleeding tendency: This statement is correct. Type 2 von Willebrand disease is characterized by a qualitative defect in von Willebrand factor, which leads to a moderate bleeding tendency. This type includes several subtypes with different defects in the von Willebrand factor."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Type 3 von Willebrand disease is associated with a complete absence of factor VIII: This statement is partially correct but misleading. Type 3 von Willebrand disease is associated with a severe deficiency or absence of von Willebrand factor, not factor VIII. However, since von Willebrand factor stabilizes factor VIII, the absence of vWF can lead to very low levels of factor VIII."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Type 2 von Willebrand disease includes subtypes with varying defects in von Willebrand factor: This statement is correct. Type 2 von Willebrand disease includes several subtypes (e.g., 2A, 2B, 2M, 2N), each with different defects affecting the function of von Willebrand factor."}]},{"n":2,"q":"Mr. X, a 46-year-old male, presents to the outpatient department with a history of lower back pain and painful urination. Upon investigation, he is found to have normocytic normochromic anemia and hypercalcemia. Serum protein electrophoresis reveals an M-spike in the gamma region. Which of the following tests is most appropriate to confirm the diagnosis of multiple myeloma?","o":[{"t":"Liver function test"},{"t":"Bence Jones protein in urine"},{"t":"Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR) test"},{"t":"Heat and acetic acid test"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Bence Jones protein in urine:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This test is critical for confirming multiple myeloma, as Bence Jones proteins are monoclonal light chains found in the urine of many patients with this condition."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The diagnosis of multiple myeloma is typically confirmed by a combination of clinical features and specific diagnostic tests. The essential tests and findings for diagnosing multiple myeloma include: Bone marrow biopsy: Shows plasmacytosis with plasma cell percentage often >10% or >60% depending on the clinical context. Serum ProteinElectrophoresis: Demonstrates the presence of an M-spike, indicating monoclonalgammopathy. Urinary Protein Electrophoresis: Helps identify Bence Jones proteins, whichare light chains of immunoglobulins found in the urine of patients with multiple myeloma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bone marrow biopsy: Shows plasmacytosis with plasma cell percentage often >10% or >60% depending on the clinical context."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum Protein Electrophoresis: Demonstrates the presence of an M-spike, indicating monoclonal gammopathy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Urinary Protein Electrophoresis: Helps identify Bence Jones proteins, which are light chains of immunoglobulins found in the urine of patients with multiple myeloma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bone marrow biopsy: Shows plasmacytosis with plasma cell percentage often >10% or >60% depending on the clinical context."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum Protein Electrophoresis: Demonstrates the presence of an M-spike, indicating monoclonal gammopathy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Urinary Protein Electrophoresis: Helps identify Bence Jones proteins, which are light chains of immunoglobulins found in the urine of patients with multiple myeloma."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Liver function test: While liver function tests can be useful in assessing overall liver health, they are not specific for diagnosing multiple myeloma."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR) test: This test is used to assess kidney function and proteinuria, but it is not specific for multiple myeloma."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Heat and acetic acid test: This test is not used for diagnosing multiple myeloma and is not relevant in this context."}]},{"n":3,"q":"A young woman presents with recurrent headaches, easy bruising, and episodes of visual blurring. Her platelet count is elevated, while her red blood cell (RBC) and white blood cell (WBC) counts are normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Essential Thrombocytosis"},{"t":"Polycythemia Vera"},{"t":"Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML)"},{"t":"Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML)"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Essential Thrombocytosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This condition is characterized by an elevated platelet count without an increase in RBC or WBC. It commonly presents with symptoms such as headaches, easy bruising, and visual disturbances. The elevated platelet count is the key diagnostic feature."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Polycythemia Vera : This condition involves elevated levels of all three blood cell types—RBCs, WBCs, and platelets. Since the RBC and WBC counts are normal in this case, polycythemia vera is less likely."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML): In CML, there is usually an elevation in granulocytes (a type of WBC) and often an increase in platelets as well. The normal WBC count in this case makes CML an unlikely diagnosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML): AML typically presents with abnormal levels of blood cells and can show varying counts of WBCs, RBCs, and platelets, but the normal RBC and WBC counts make AML less likely in this scenario."}]},{"n":4,"q":"A patient presents with symptoms of fatigue, pallor, and weakness. Laboratory tests reveal a low hemoglobin (Hb) level, but the reticulocyte count is normal. Based on these findings, which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Aplastic Anemia"},{"t":"Pure Red Cell Aplasia"},{"t":"Iron Deficiency Anemia"},{"t":"Hemolytic Anemia"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Pure Red Cell Aplasia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This condition is characterized by a specific reduction in red blood cells while the white blood cells and platelets remain normal. The normal reticulocyte count in this case suggests that the bone marrow is not producing red blood cells despite the body's need, aligning with pure red cell aplasia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Aplastic Anemia: In aplastic anemia, you would typically observe pancytopenia (decreased levels of red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets) due to a failure of the bone marrow. The normal reticulocyte count seen in this patient does not support this diagnosis, as one would expect low reticulocyte counts in aplastic anemia due to ineffective hematopoiesis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Iron Deficiency Anemia : Iron deficiency anemia typically presents with microcytic, hypochromic red blood cells and often an increased reticulocyte count as the bone marrow responds to low hemoglobin levels. In this scenario, there is no indication of microcytic red blood cells or elevated reticulocytes, making this diagnosis less likely."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Hemolytic Anemia : Hemolytic anemia is associated with increased reticulocyte counts due to the bone marrow's response to the destruction of red blood cells. The normal reticulocyte count in this case does not fit the pattern of hemolytic anemia."}]},{"n":5,"q":"Which of the following is incorrect regarding myelodysplastic syndrome?","o":[{"t":"More commonly seen in females"},{"t":"Treatment-induced MDS is not dependent on age"},{"t":"Median age of onset 70 years"},{"t":"It can be radiation-induced"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - More commonly seen in females:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MDS is actually more commonly seen in males than in females."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C, and D are true regarding myelodysplastic syndrome."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The median age of onset for MDS is around 70 years, making it primarily a disease of the elderly. It can be induced by radiation exposure, either as a part of cancer treatment or from other sources of significant radiation exposure. Treatment-induced MDS is not dependent on age."}]},{"n":6,"q":"26 years old female presents with menorrhagia and excessive bleeding after minor surgery. She gave the history of father and sister having same bleeding issues and pattern of inheritance was determined to be autosomal dominant. What is the most likely disorder?","o":[{"t":"Von Wilebrand disease"},{"t":"Hemophilia A"},{"t":"Christmas disease"},{"t":"Factor 10 deficiency"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Von Wilebrand disease:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is a more common cause of bleeding and follows the Autosomal dominant pattern of inheritance. The given scenario also shows autosomal dominant inheritance pattern as father and her sister is also having same bleeding problem"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Hemophilia A and Option C - Christmas disease: Christmas diseases are X linked recessive and are ruled out."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Factor 10 deficiency: Have both autosomal dominant and recessive inheritance but AD pattern of inheritance and is less common in Factor 10 deficiency."}]},{"n":7,"q":"Which of the following statements is correct regarding polycythemia vera?","o":[{"t":"Risk of thrombosis strongly correlates with the degree of thrombocytosis"},{"t":"Generalized pruritus is a consequence of mast cell activation by JAK 2 mutation"},{"t":"It is protective against H. Pylori infection"},{"t":"Acute myeloid leukaemia is the most common cause of mortality"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Generalized pruritus is a consequence of mast cell activation by JAK2 mutation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In polycythemia vera, a mutation in the JAK2 gene (JAK2 V617F mutation) is commonly observed. This mutation leads to the overactivation of certain cells, including mast cells, which release mediators that can cause generalized pruritus (itching) as a characteristic symptom of the disease."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Other clinical features include high haemoglobin, or with symptoms of hyperviscosity, such as lassitude, loss of concentration, headaches, dizziness, blackouts, pruritus and epistaxis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Option:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Risk of thrombosis strongly correlates with the degree of thrombocytosis: While polycythemia vera is associated with an increased risk of thrombosis, the degree of thrombocytosis alone does not strongly correlate with the risk.sk."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - It is protective against H. pylori infection: Polycythemia vera does not confer protection against Helicobacter pylori (H. pylori) infection."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Acute myeloid leukemia is the most common cause of mortality: While patients with polycythemia vera have an increased risk of developing acute myeloid leukemia (AML) and other myeloproliferative neoplasms, it is not the most common cause of mortality in polycythemia vera."}]},{"n":8,"q":"What is the treatment for neutropenia following chemotherapy?","o":[{"t":"Leucovorin"},{"t":"Filgastrim"},{"t":"Ondansetron"},{"t":"Darbepoetin"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Filgastrim:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Filgrastim: Filgrastim is a granulocyte colony-stimulating factor (G-CSF) that stimulates the production of neutrophils. It is commonly used to treat neutropenia caused by chemotherapy by promoting the production of new neutrophils in the bone marrow."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is started one day after myelosuppressive chemotherapy and injected SC or IV, 5 µg/kg daily."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Leucovorin: Leucovorin is a medication that is used in combination with certain chemotherapy drugs to enhance their effectiveness. It is not specifically indicated for the treatment of neutropenia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Ondansetron: Ondansetron is an antiemetic medication used to prevent nausea and vomiting, particularly associated with chemotherapy or radiation treatment. It does not directly address neutropenia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Darbepoetin: Darbepoetin is a synthetic form of erythropoietin, a hormone that stimulates the production of red blood cells. It is used to treat anemia, but it does not have a direct effect on neutropenia."}]},{"n":9,"q":"A patient of haemophilia received multiple blood transfusions. Which of the following metabolic abnormalities can be seen in a patient?","o":[{"t":"Metabolic alkalosis"},{"t":"Respiratory alkalosis"},{"t":"Metabolic acidosis"},{"t":"Respiratory acidosis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Metabolic alkalosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In patients with haemophilia who receive multiple blood transfusions, metabolic alkalosis can occur as a result of the citrate anticoagulant used in stored blood products."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Citrate toxicity causes raised pH leading to redistribution of potassium eventually causing metabolic alkalosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Option:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Respiratory alkalosis: Respiratory alkalosis is characterized by a decrease in arterial carbon dioxide (CO2) levels, resulting in an increase in blood pH."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Metabolic acidosis: Metabolic acidosis is characterized by a decrease in blood pH and bicarbonate levels."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Respiratory acidosis: Respiratory acidosis is characterized by an increase in arterial carbon dioxide (CO2) levels, resulting in a decrease in blood pH."}]},{"n":10,"q":"A 30-year-old female with a history of recurrent headaches presented for further evaluation. On further workup, hemoglobin and total WBC count were normal, while an elevated platelet count was seen. Bone marrow aspiration showed increased megakaryocytes and a JAK2 mutation. Which is the most likely diagnosis?\n(or)\nA 30-year-old female with a history of recurrent headaches presented for further evaluation. On further workup, hemoglobin and total WBC count were normal, while an elevated platelet count was seen. Bone marrow aspiration showed increased megakaryocytes and a JAK2 mutation. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Polycythemia vera"},{"t":"Essential thrombocytosis"},{"t":"Primary myelofibrosis"},{"t":"Chronic myelogenous leukemia"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Essential thrombocytosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The most probable diagnosis is essential thrombocytosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is a clonal hematopoietic stem cell disorder causing uncontrolled proliferation of megakaryocytes results in a raised level of circulating platelets that are often dysfunctional."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presence of JAK-2, CALR or rarely, MPL mutation and normal Hb and WBC level along with increased platelet count supports the diagnosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Polycythemia vera"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Patients have raised hemoglobin levels in polycythemia vera."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Primary myelofibrosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bone marrow yields dry tap in primary myelofibrosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Chronic myelogenous leukemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CML has a Philadelphia chromosome due to reciprocal translocation t (9;22) and hypercellular marrow."}]},{"n":11,"q":"Which test is utilized for the diagnosis of DIC among the following options?","o":[{"t":"Fibrin degradation products"},{"t":"Activation partial thromboplastin time"},{"t":"Prothrombin time"},{"t":"Dimer assay"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Dimer assay:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"D-dimer assay is a blood test used to detect the presence of D-dimer, a fibrin degradation product that is elevated in conditions associated with fibrin formation and breakdown, such as DIC."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Elevated D-dimer levels indicate the activation of the clotting system and can support the diagnosis of DIC."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Fibrin degradation products is incorrect because while the presence of fibrin degradation products is a characteristic feature of DIC, the specific test used for their detection is the D-dimer assay."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Activated partial thromboplastin time is incorrect because activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) is a coagulation test that measures the intrinsic pathway of coagulation and is not specific to the diagnosis of DIC."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Prothrombin time is incorrect because prothrombin time (PT) is a coagulation test that measures the extrinsic pathway of coagulation and is not specific to the diagnosis of DIC."}]},{"n":12,"q":"What is the probable diagnosis for a girl who experiences prolonged menstrual bleeding and has normal platelet count, PT, and aPTT, but an increased bleeding time, which was determined to be due to a deficiency of GpIIb-IIIa after further analysis?","o":[{"t":"Bernard Soulier syndrome"},{"t":"Glanzmann thrombasthenia"},{"t":"Haemophilia A"},{"t":"Von Willebrand disease"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Glanzmann thrombasthenia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The above case is that of Glanzmann thrombasthenia which is an autosomal recessive platelet surface disorder of GPIIb/IIIa."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It has normal platelet morphology, prolonged BT, absent or decreased clot retraction, and normal platelet aggregation in the presence of ristocetin."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Bernard Soulier syndrome is an inherited deficiency of Ib-IX in which platelet count is decreased, BT is increased, PT is normal, and aPTT is normal."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Haemophilia A is a hereditary bleeding disorder due to a deficiency of factor VIII."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Von Willebrand disease is due to qualitative and quantitative defects in VWF in which platelet count is usually normal, BT is increased, PT is normal, and aPTT is increased."}]},{"n":13,"q":"Adhesion of platelets is the main function of von-willebrand factor. Which of the following is true for von-willebrand disease?","o":[{"t":"Normal partial thromboplastin time"},{"t":"Decreased platelets"},{"t":"Normal prothrombin time"},{"t":"Normal bleeding time"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Normal prothrombin time:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Von Willebrand disease (VWD) is a bleeding disorder caused by a deficiency or dysfunction of von Willebrand factor (VWF), a protein involved in platelet adhesion and the stabilization of factor VIII in the blood clotting cascade."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prothrombin time is not affected in von-willebrand disease as von-willebrand factor affects only the intrinsic factor."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - \"Normal partial thromboplastin time (PTT)\": PTT is prolonged in von-willebrand disease as it does not bind to factor 8."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - \"Decreased platelets\": Von Willebrand disease does not directly affect platelet production or cause a decrease in platelet count. Platelet count is generally normal in VWD. Therefore, option B is incorrect."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - \"Normal bleeding time\": Since von Willebrand factor plays a crucial role in platelet adhesion, patients with VWD may have an increased bleeding time due to impaired platelet function. Therefore, option D is incorrect."}]},{"n":14,"q":"Translocation of lymphoma asked in match the following Column A Column B 1. Burkitt lymphoma 2. Mantle cell lymphoma 3. Marginal cell lymphoma 4. Follicular lymphoma 5. Ewing’s sarcoma A. t(11,18) B. t(14,18) C. t(8,14) D. t(11,22) E. t(11,14)\nColumn A Column B\n1. Burkitt lymphoma A. t(11,18) 2. Mantle cell lymphoma B. t(14,18) 3. Marginal cell lymphoma C. t(8,14) 4. Follicular lymphoma D. t(11,22) 5. Ewing’s sarcoma E. t(11,14)","o":[{"t":"1-A, 2-E, 3-C, 4-B, 5-D"},{"t":"1-C, 2-D, 3-A, 4-B, 5-E"},{"t":"1-C, 2-E, 3-A, 4-D, 5-B"},{"t":"1-C, 2-E, 3-A, 4-B, 5-D"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D: 1-C, 2-E, 3-A, 4-B, 5-D:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Burkitt lymphoma t(8,14)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Mantle cell lymphoma t(11,14)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Marginal cell lymphoma t(11,18)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Follicular lymphoma t(14,18)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Ewing's sarcoma t(11,22)"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options: Options A, B, and C are incorrect."}]},{"n":15,"q":"Which type of cancer is not linked to viral infection among the options provided?","o":[{"t":"Kaposi sarcoma"},{"t":"Primary effusion lymphoma"},{"t":"Merkel cell carcinoma"},{"t":"Superficial spreading melanoma"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Superficial spreading melanoma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Superficial spreading melanoma is a type of skin cancer that primarily occurs due to exposure to ultraviolet (UV) radiation from the sun or artificial sources, such as tanning beds. It is not directly associated with viral infections like the other options."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Option:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Kaposi sarcoma- Kaposi sarcoma is caused by human herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8) infection. It commonly affects individuals with weakened immune systems, such as those with HIV/AIDS."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Primary effusion lymphoma- Primary effusion lymphoma is associated with human herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8) infection. It primarily occurs in individuals with compromised immune systems, such as those with HIV/AIDS."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Merkel cell carcinoma- Merkel cell carcinoma is associated with Merkel cell polyomavirus (MCV) infection. This virus is thought to play a role in the development of Merkel cell carcinoma, a rare and aggressive form of skin cancer."}]},{"n":16,"q":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the diagnosis of accelerated phase of chronic myeloid leukemia (CML)?\n(or)\nMark the false statement regarding diagnosis of accelerated phase of CML:","o":[{"t":"Leukocytosis more than 1000 ×10 6 not responding to treatment"},{"t":"Blast concentration in PBS should be greater than 15%"},{"t":"Increased spleen size not responsive to therapy"},{"t":"No response to tyrosine kinase inhibitor"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Leukocytosis more than 1000 × 10^ 6 not responding to treatment:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Leukocytosis more than 1000 × 10^6 is actually a characteristic feature of the accelerated phase of CML. However, it's important to note that the response to treatment can vary, and the lack of response to treatment is not specific to the diagnosis of the accelerated phase of CML."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Blast concentration in PBS should be greater than 15%"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This statement is true. Blast concentration in PBS should be greater than 15% is one of the diagnostic criteria for accelerated phase of CML."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Increased spleen size not responsive to therapy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This statement is true. In the accelerated phase of CML, the spleen typically becomes enlarged (splenomegaly), and this enlargement may not respond adequately to therapy."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - No response to tyrosine kinase inhibitor."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This statement is true. In the accelerated phase of CML, there may be a reduced response or lack of response to tyrosine kinase inhibitor (TKI) therapy, which is the standard treatment for CML."}]},{"n":17,"q":"In which of the following conditions will the pneumococcal vaccine PPV-23 provide the greatest advantage?","o":[{"t":"Recurrent rhinitis and sinusitis"},{"t":"Cystic fibrosis"},{"t":"Sickle cell anemia"},{"t":"Child <2 years"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Sickle cell anemia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pneumococcal vaccine PPV-23, is recommended to prevent pneumococcal infections in individuals with sickle cell anemia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Recurrent rhinitis and sinusitis: While recurrent rhinitis and sinusitis may be troublesome conditions, they are not specifically indications for the pneumococcal vaccine PPV-23."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Cystic fibrosis: Specific guidelines and recommendations for vaccination in individuals with cystic fibrosis, and the choice of pneumococcal vaccine (PPV-13 or PPV-23) may depend on various factors, including age and previous vaccination history."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Child <2 years: The PPV-23 vaccine is typically given to individuals aged 2 years and older who are at increased risk of invasive pneumococcal disease."}]},{"n":18,"q":"A patient was transfused 4 units of blood 2 hours ago, and now she complains of chest discomfort. The chest X-ray shows bilateral fissural thickening. Which of the following is the most probable cause?","o":[{"t":"ABO incompatibility reactions"},{"t":"Fluid overload"},{"t":"Hypocalcemia"},{"t":"Graft-versus-host disease"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Fluid overload:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient has chest discomfort after transfusing 4 units of blood, and a chest X-ray showing bilateral fissural thickening is suggestive of fluid overload."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Transfusion related circulatory overload is due to fast administration of blood products."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options A - ABO incompatibility reactions"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ABO incompatibility reactions is characterized by fever, nausea, hypo or hypertension."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Hypocalcemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypocalcemia is feature of massive blood transfusion."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Graft vs Host reaction"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Graft vs Host reaction presents with fever, diarrhea, jaundice, pruritus"}]},{"n":19,"q":"A 45-year-old presented with severe pain in the right leg below the knee. He had a history of restricted ambulation and prolonged bed rest due to severe back pain. He has no other comorbidities. On examination, the skin over his right calf appears shiny and taut. Additionally, tenderness and increased girth of the right calf are also noted. What is the most probable clinical diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Cellulitis of the leg"},{"t":"Compartment syndrome"},{"t":"Deep vein thrombosis"},{"t":"Arterial claudication"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Deep vein thrombosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The above case scenario is diagnostic of Deep vein thrombosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pain and swelling of the calf region is the most common symptom of DVT and calf tenderness is the most common sign."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":20,"q":"What immunological abnormality is observed due to the absence of CD40 in B cells?","o":[{"t":"Total lack of NK cells"},{"t":"Lack of CD8 mediated cytotoxicity"},{"t":"Decreased IgG and increase in IgM"},{"t":"Inability of neutrophil to act against infections"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Decreased IgG and increase in IgM:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hyper IgM syndrome is a rare B cell primary immunodeficiency disorder characterized by defective Ig CSR."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It results in very low serum levels of IgG and IgA and elevated or normal serum IgM levels."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The lack of CD40 in B cells results in decreased IgG and IgA production, and an increase in IgM production, which leads to an immunological abnormality characterized by a decreased ability to mount an effective immune response against infections."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":21,"q":"A 20-year-old female patient with early satiety has a spleen palpable till the umbilicus along with petechiae and bleeding. The WBC count is 85k/cu.mm with immature B-cells seen in peripheral blood smear. The diagnosis is?","o":[{"t":"AML"},{"t":"CML"},{"t":"CLL"},{"t":"ALL"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - CML:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chronic myeloid leukemia occurs due to the translocation of Philadelphia chromosome t(9:22)(q34.1, q11.2)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In the above-given scenario, massive splenomegaly is a differentiating feature that helps diagnose CML."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Patients present with involuntary weight loss, fatigue, sweating."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other features: Marked Basophilia Pruritis Progressive constipation Total cell count is elevated BCR-ABL-1 leads to an increase in the platelet count. Platelet count is decreased in blast crisis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Marked Basophilia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pruritis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Progressive constipation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Total cell count is elevated"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"BCR-ABL-1 leads to an increase in the platelet count."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Platelet count is decreased in blast crisis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Marked Basophilia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pruritis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Progressive constipation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Total cell count is elevated"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"BCR-ABL-1 leads to an increase in the platelet count."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Platelet count is decreased in blast crisis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - AML:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"AML presents with an extremely high TLC count contributing to leukostasis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - CLL:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CLL is the most common leukemia in geriatric patients that presents with splenomegaly and progressive cervical lymphadenopathy"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - ALL:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ALL can cross BBB to form CNS leukemia and features of meningitis and raise ICP"}]},{"n":22,"q":"Which of the following mutations is associated with the Philadelphia chromosome?","o":[{"t":"Inversion"},{"t":"Insertion"},{"t":"Reciprocal translocation"},{"t":"Deletion"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C- Reciprocal translocation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The Philadelphia chromosome is the result of a specific reciprocal translocation between chromosomes 9 and 22, designated t(9;22)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This translocation leads to the formation of the BCR-ABL fusion gene, which is associated with chronic myeloid leukemia and some cases of acute lymphoblastic leukemia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A- Inversion: An inversion mutation occurs when a segment of DNA breaks away from the chromosome, flips around, and reattaches to the chromosome in the reverse orientation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B- Insertion: An insertion mutation involves the addition of extra nucleotides into a DNA sequence. It can result in a frameshift mutation or alter the coding sequence of a gene."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D- Deletion: A deletion mutation involves the loss of a segment of DNA from a chromosome."}]},{"n":23,"q":"What would be the most appropriate course of action for managing a patient presenting with fatigue, a haemoglobin level below 9 g/dl, and sickle-shaped red blood cells on peripheral smear examination?","o":[{"t":"Administer IV antibiotics"},{"t":"Blood transfusion"},{"t":"Administer deferoxamine"},{"t":"Wait and watch"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Blood transfusion:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient’s presentation with fatigue, a low haemoglobin level, and sickle-shaped red blood cells on peripheral smear examination is suggestive of sickle cell disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sickle cell anemia is a genetic disorder that affects the shape and function of red blood cells, causing them to become rigid and form a sickle shape, which can lead to various complications."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Blood transfusion is a common treatment option for sickle cell anemia patients with symptomatic anemia or acute complications."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Transfusion therapy aims to increase the hemoglobin level and decrease the percentage of sickle-shaped cells in circulation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options A, C, and D are not the appropriate treatment for sickle cell anemia."}],"qi":[7405]},{"n":24,"q":"Which statement is accurate regarding paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH)?","o":[{"t":"Inherited defect in PIG-A"},{"t":"Extravascular haemolysis"},{"t":"Deficiency of CD 55 and CD 59"},{"t":"Microcytic anaemia"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Deficiency of CD55 and CD59:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"PNH is associated with a deficiency of CD55 (decay-accelerating factor) and CD59 (membrane inhibitor of reactive lysis), which are GPI-anchored proteins that regulate the complement system and protect blood cells from complement-mediated destruction."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Inherited defect in PIG-A. Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH) is caused by a somatic mutation in the PIG-A gene, which is an acquired defect."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - PNH is characterized by intravascular hemolysis , not extravascular hemolysis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - PNH is not typically associated with microcytic anemia ."}]},{"n":25,"q":"An elderly woman presented with symptoms of confusion, thirst, and abdominal pain. On examination, she had pallor and thoracic spine tenderness. Her lab investigations showed the following findings. An x-ray of her skull is shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis? Hb-6.9g/dl WBC 4000/cm3 with normal differential count Serum calcium 13mg/dl Creatinine 2.3mg/dl Albumin 2.4g/dl Urinalysis: positive for Bence-Jones proteins","o":[{"t":"Metastatic breast cancer"},{"t":"Multiple myeloma"},{"t":"Primary hyperparathyroidism"},{"t":"Milk alkali syndrome"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B. Multiple myeloma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Multiple myeloma, also known as plasma celldyscrasia, presents with CRAB features C- Hypercalcemia R- Renal failure A- Anemia B- Bleeding"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"C- Hypercalcemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"R- Renal failure"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A- Anemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"B- Bleeding"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The X-ray of the skull showing lytic lesions- Classical rain drop configuration."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"C- Hypercalcemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"R- Renal failure"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A- Anemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"B- Bleeding"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Incorrect choices:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Option A: Metastatic breast cancer: Metastasis of breast cancer to the bones can cause hypercalcemia, bone pain, and pathological fractures."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Option C: Primary hyperparathyroidism is a condition in which there is an overproduction of parathyroid hormone, leading to hypercalcemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Option D: Milk alkali syndrome is a condition that can occur due to excessive intake of calcium and absorbable alkali. This can lead to hypercalcemia and metabolic alkalosis."}],"qi":[7411]},{"n":26,"q":"In which of the following conditions is elective splenectomy the preferred treatment option?","o":[{"t":"G6PD deficiency-Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency"},{"t":"Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria"},{"t":"Hereditary spherocytosis"},{"t":"Hairy cell leukemia"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hereditary spherocytosis is an inherited disorder characterized by abnormal red blood cells that are spherical in shape instead of the normal disc shape."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Treatment of HS Severe HS- Elective splenectomy (4-6 years) Moderate HS- Elective splenectomy delayed upto puberty Mild HS- Folic acid supplementation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Severe HS- Elective splenectomy (4-6 years)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Moderate HS- Elective splenectomy delayed upto puberty"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mild HS- Folic acid supplementation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Severe HS- Elective splenectomy (4-6 years)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Moderate HS- Elective splenectomy delayed upto puberty"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mild HS- Folic acid supplementation"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Choices:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Option A: In G6PD deficiency, splenectomy may worsen the anaemia in these individuals because the spleen filters and removes damaged red blood cells from the circulation ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Option B : In Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH) there is an abnormality in the complement system. Stem cell transplant is the best treatment."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Option D: In Hairy cell leukemia, Chemotherapy is the standard treatment for hairy cell leukemia. Splenectomy may be considered massively enlarged spleen or if there is resistance to other treatments."}]},{"n":27,"q":"What is the type of inheritance seen in sickle cell anemia?","o":[{"t":"AD"},{"t":"AR"},{"t":"X-linked dominant"},{"t":"X-linked recessive"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct option:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sickle cell anemia is a genetic disorder caused by the mislocation of valine in the position of glutamate at the b-6 position of the Haemoglobin gene."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is an inherited autosomal recessive disorder as the individual is symptomatic, but the parents each have a copy of the defective gene and are asymptomatic."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A. In an autosomal dominant disease, every individual with a copy of the gene is symptomatic. The trait, as such, is expressed in every individual carrying the defective gene."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C. In X-linked dominant disorders, mutations are passed down from parents via a single X chromosome. In a female with a normal X and a mutated X chromosome, the single mutated chromosome is enough to express the condition."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D . In X-linked recessive disorders, mutations are passed down from parents via a single X chromosome, such that a male carrying only one X chromosome is affected while a female carrying a normal X and a mutated X chromosome is usually unaffected."}]},{"n":28,"q":"Which conditions are associated with thrombocytopenia, eczema, and recurrent infections?","o":[{"t":"Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome"},{"t":"Chediak-Higashi syndrome"},{"t":"Thrombocytopenia – absent radius syndrome"},{"t":"Hermansky-Pudlak syndrome"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome is a rare genetic disorder characterized by the triad of thrombocytopenia (low platelet count), eczema (skin rash), and recurrent infections."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is an X-linked disorder that primarily affects males, although there have been rare cases reported in females."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Chediak-Higashi syndrome: Chediak-Higashi syndrome is a rare autosomal recessive disorder characterized by abnormal immune system function and abnormal pigmentation of the skin and hair."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Thrombocytopenia – absent radius syndrome: Thrombocytopenia – absent radius syndrome (TAR syndrome) is a rare genetic disorder characterized by low platelet count (thrombocytopenia) and the absence or underdevelopment of the radius bone in the forearm."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Hermansky-Pudlak syndrome: Hermansky-Pudlak syndrome is a rare genetic disorder characterized by oculocutaneous albinism, bleeding problems due to platelet dysfunction, and various other symptoms."}]},{"n":29,"q":"What is the probable diagnosis for a ten-year-old boy who presents with fever, hand swelling, and a history of recurrent hand swelling episodes, along with imaging showing a contracted spleen?","o":[{"t":"Sickle cell anaemia"},{"t":"Malaria"},{"t":"Pancreatitis"},{"t":"Measles"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Sickle cell anaemia:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Sickle cell anemia is an inherited blood disorder characterized by the presence of abnormal hemoglobin called hemoglobin S. The RBCs become rigid and are trapped in splenic capillaries."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"USG abdomen findings in sickle cell anemia: Shrunken spleen Hypersplenism may be present initial."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Shrunken spleen"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypersplenism may be present initial."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hand X-ray shows dactylitis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Shrunken spleen"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypersplenism may be present initial."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Malaria: While malaria can cause splenomegaly (enlarged spleen), it does not typically lead to splenic atrophy."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Pancreatitis: Pancreatitis is inflammation of the pancreas and is not associated with fever, hand swelling, or splenic atrophy."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Measles: Measles is a viral infection that primarily affects the respiratory system. It does not cause splenic atrophy or hand swelling."}]},{"n":30,"q":"A 68-year-old male came to the physician with complaints of chronic back pain, easy fatigability, and a past history of multiple urinary tract infections in the last 3 months. His ESR is elevated, x-ray of his skull is shown below. Which of the following investigations is useful for diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"MRI brain"},{"t":"PET scan"},{"t":"Head CT scan with contrast"},{"t":"Serum electrophoresis"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Serum electrophoresis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient's presentation with chronic back pain and fatiguability with elveated ESR and a skull x-ray showing multiple punched-out lytic lesions is suggestive of multiple myeloma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Screening tests for the diagnosis of multiple myeloma: Serum protein electrophoresis > urine protein electrophoresis - Shows spike in monoclonal M-protein"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum protein electrophoresis > urine protein electrophoresis - Shows spike in monoclonal M-protein"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum protein electrophoresis > urine protein electrophoresis - Shows spike in monoclonal M-protein"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options: Options A, B, and C are incorrect. Refer to the explanation of the correct answer."}],"qi":[7411]},{"n":31,"q":"What is the diagnosis of a 50-year-old man who presents with symptoms of anemia, fever, and recurrent nosebleeds? The patient has hepatosplenomegaly and lab investigations indicate a hemoglobin level of 6g/dL, a total leukocyte count of 40,000/cu.mm with 10% blasts, and a platelet count of 12,000/cu.mm. Further investigation reveals the presence of t(8;21) in the blasts.","o":[{"t":"Acute lymphoblastic leukemia"},{"t":"Acute myeloblastic leukemia"},{"t":"Preleukemia"},{"t":"Aleukemic leukemia"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Acute myeloblastic leukemia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The above is the case of AML as it has t(8;21) ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Work up of AML Blast concentration <20% in peripheral blood/bone marrow aspiration. Decreased Hb Decreased platelet count Decreased TLC initially Demonstration of auer rods in bone marrow"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Blast concentration <20% in peripheral blood/bone marrow aspiration."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Decreased Hb"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Decreased platelet count"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Decreased TLC initially"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Demonstration of auer rods in bone marrow"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Blast concentration <20% in peripheral blood/bone marrow aspiration."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Decreased Hb"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Decreased platelet count"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Decreased TLC initially"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Demonstration of auer rods in bone marrow"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - ALL is the most common childhood leukaemia having symptoms such as fever, night sweats unexplained weight loss painless lymphadenopathy bone pain which are not present here."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Pre-leukaemia Is a myelodysplastic syndrome"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Aleukemic leukaemia Is a rare type of leukaemia in which the leucocyte count is normal or to the lower side."}]},{"n":32,"q":"Which of the following investigations should be conducted in a 7-month-old child who has a hemoglobin level of 6.3, a history of recurrent blood transfusions, an MCV of 70 fl, normal MCH, normal MCHC, and a peripheral smear indicating a hypochromic microcytic image?","o":[{"t":"Serum iron"},{"t":"Direct coomb’s test"},{"t":"Osmotic fragility test"},{"t":"Hb electrophoresis"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Hb electrophoresis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The above case is most probably a case of thalassemia as there is a history of repeated transfusions, low MCV and low Hb and the peripheral smear shows a picture that is hypochromic microcytic. The next best step is to get Hb electrophoresis done."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hb electrophoresis indicates elevated levels of HbF and HbA2."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Serum iron is measured to check for iron overload due to repeated blood transfusions and is not the next best step."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Direct coomb’s test is for autoimmune haemolytic anemia and not for thalassemia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Osmotic fragility test is done for hereditary spherocytosis."}]},{"n":33,"q":"A 30-year-old female patient complains of tiredness and shows signs of paleness during physical examination. The patient's lab results are as follows: Hemoglobin level - 9.8 g/dL, Platelet count - 1,80,000/m3, Leukocyte count - 7800/mm3, Mean Corpuscular Volume (MCV) - 160 fl. Serum methyl malonyl CoA levels are within the normal range. Based on these findings, which therapy should be administered to this patient?","o":[{"t":"Vitamin B12"},{"t":"Iron"},{"t":"Vitamin C"},{"t":"Folic acid"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Folic acid:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The above given scenario is diagnostic of Folate deficiency."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Oral folic acid (5mg daily for 3 weeks) will treat acute deficiency and 5 mg once weekly is adequate for maintenance therapy"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - While Vitamin B12 deficiency can cause macrocytic anemia (high MCV), it is not the most likely cause in this case because the serum methyl malonyl CoA levels are normal."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Iron deficiency anemia can cause microcytic (small-sized) anemia, but the laboratory values provided indicate a high MCV, which is inconsistent with iron deficiency anemia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Vitamin C deficiency is not typically associated with anemia or elevated MCV."}]},{"n":34,"q":"What is the most appropriate treatment strategy for a 25-year-old patient who presents with symptoms of chronic diarrhea, fatigue, tiredness, and exhibits the nail findings shown below, with a hemoglobin level of 8 gm%?","o":[{"t":"Folic acid"},{"t":"Metronidazole"},{"t":"Iron supplements"},{"t":"Vitamins B12"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Iron supplements:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The nail findings shown in the image might indicate koilonychia, which is characterized by spoon-shaped or concave nails."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Koilonychia can be associated with iron deficiency anemia. Chronic diarrhea, fatigue, tiredness, and a low hemoglobin level (anemia) can also be signs of iron deficiency. The best treatment approach would be Iron supplements."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Folic acid is typically used to address folic acid deficiency anemia, which can also cause fatigue and tiredness."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Metronidazole is an antibiotic used to treat certain types of infections, particularly those caused by anaerobic bacteria and protozoa."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Vitamin B12 is used to treat vitamin B12 deficiency anemia, which can cause fatigue and tiredness."}],"qi":[7418]},{"n":35,"q":"A 47-year-old man with a diagnosis of acute myeloid leukemia with a blood type O negative presents to the transplant clinic to discuss proceeding with an allogeneic stem cell transplant. Which of the following would be an optimal donor?","o":[{"t":"His brother"},{"t":"Umbilical cord transplant"},{"t":"His HLA twin identical brother who is otherwise healthy and is blood type O+"},{"t":"An HLA identical matched unrelated donor"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - His HLA twin identical brother who is otherwise healthy and is blood type O+:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The correct option would be the patient's HLA identical brother who is otherwise healthy and has blood type O+. HLA matching is a crucial factor in determining the compatibility of the donor for an allogeneic stem cell transplant."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"As long as the HLA types match, the patient can receive stem cells from a donor with a different blood type without significant adverse effects."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - His brother (Incorrect)- HLA- identical twin brother have the best results with allogenic stem cell transplant While an identical twin brother is a perfect genetic match, an allogeneic stem cell transplant refers to receiving stem cells from a donor who is not an identical twin. Therefore, an identical twin brother would not be considered an optimal donor in this case."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Umbilical cord transplant (Incorrect) Umbilical cord blood can be a source of stem cells for transplantation, but the patient's blood type O negative may limit the availability of compatible cord blood units."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - An HLA identical matched unrelated donor who(Incorrect) The information regarding the blood type of the unrelated donor is not provided. Without knowing the blood type compatibility, it is difficult to determine the suitability of this option."}]},{"n":36,"q":"A 60-year-old man presents with fatigue, pallor, weight loss, and heaviness in the left hypochondrium, for the past 2 months. Abdominal palpation reveals splenomegaly. His hemogram and peripheral smear results are given below. What is the most likely diagnosis? Hemogram: Hb-10 gm/dl WBC count – 1,65,000/cu.mm (leukocytosis) Platelet count – 3 lakhs/mm3 Peripheral smear: Metamyelocytes – 15% Promyeloblasts – 30% Myeloblasts – 45%","o":[{"t":"Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"},{"t":"Chronic myeloid leukemia"},{"t":"Acute lymphoblastic leukemia"},{"t":"Acute myeloid leukemia"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The most likely diagnosis for the patient in this scenario is chronic myeloid leukemia (CML)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient has leukocytosis with a very high white blood cell count (1,65,000/cu.mm), which is a hallmark of CML. Additionally, the peripheral smear shows an increase in myeloblasts, promyelocytes, and metamyelocytes, which are immature forms of white blood cells commonly seen in CML."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Incorrect choices:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Option A: Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL) is a type of leukemia that typically presents with lymphocytosis, or an increase in lymphocytes, rather than leukocytosis. The peripheral smear would also show an increase in lymphocytes rather than myeloid cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Option C: In Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) , the peripheral smear would show a predominance of lymphoblasts rather than myeloblasts."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Option D: In Acute myeloid leukemia (AML) is a type of leukemia that presents with symptoms such as fatigue, fever, and easy bruising or bleeding. AML typically presents with more acute symptoms and a shorter disease course compared to CML."}]},{"n":37,"q":"What is the most probable condition of a 10-year-old boy complaining of recurrent episodes of intense pain in his fingers and toes that spontaneously resolve over the course of the last year?","o":[{"t":"Alpha-thalassemia"},{"t":"Beta-thalassemia"},{"t":"Sickle cell anemia"},{"t":"Von Willebrand disease type 1"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Sickle cell anemia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sickle cell anemia is a genetic blood disorder characterized by the presence of abnormal hemoglobin, known as hemoglobin S ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In sickle cell anemia, the abnormal red blood cells can become stiff and sticky, leading to blockages in the blood vessels."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These blockages can cause episodes of pain called vaso-occlusive crises of sickle cell crises. These crises can occur in various parts of the body, including the bones, joints, and organs."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Alpha-thalassemia: Alpha-thalassemia is a genetic disorder characterized by reduced or absent production of alpha-globin chains. It can lead to anemia, but it typically does not cause episodes of pain or resolve on its own."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Beta-thalassemia: Beta-thalassemia is a genetic disorder characterized by reduced or absent production of beta-globin chains. Similar to alpha-thalassemia, it can cause anemia but does not typically present with episodes of pain that resolve spontaneously."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Von Willebrand disease type 1: Von Willebrand disease is a bleeding disorder caused by a deficiency or dysfunction of von Willebrand factor, a protein involved in blood clotting."}]},{"n":38,"q":"An 18-year-old girl with the diagnosis of acute promyelocytic leukemia was treated medically. She developed fever and tachypnea and a chest X-ray showed pulmonary infiltrates. What drug should she be given next?","o":[{"t":"Cytarabine"},{"t":"Dexamethasone"},{"t":"Doxorubicin"},{"t":"Methotrexate"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Dexamethasone:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this scenario, the 18-year-old girl with acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL) developed fever, tachypnea, and pulmonary infiltrates on a chest X-ray. These symptoms and findings suggest the development of a potentially serious complication called acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) associated with APL."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dexamethasone, a corticosteroid, is the appropriate choice of drug in this situation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is commonly used in the management of APL to help mitigate the differentiation syndrome, also known as retinoic acid syndrome, which can occur during the initial treatment with all-trans retinoic acid (ATRA) or arsenic trioxide (ATO)."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Cytarabine: Cytarabine is a chemotherapy drug commonly used in the treatment of acute myeloid leukemia (AML), including certain subtypes of APL."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Doxorubicin: Doxorubicin is a chemotherapy drug commonly used in the treatment of various cancers, including APL."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Methotrexate: Methotrexate is a chemotherapy drug used in the treatment of various malignancies, including certain types of leukemia."}]},{"n":39,"q":"What is the most frequently observed bleeding manifestation in individuals with severe hemophilia?","o":[{"t":"Recurrent hematomas"},{"t":"Recurrent hemarthrosis"},{"t":"Hematuria"},{"t":"Intracranial hemorrhage"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Recurrent hemarthrosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In severe hemophilia, which is characterized by a deficiency of clotting factor VIII (hemophilia A) or clotting factor IX (hemophilia B), the most common bleeding manifestation is recurrent hemarthrosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Recurrent hematomas: While hematomas can occur in individuals with severe hemophilia, they are not as common as recurrent hemarthrosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Hematuria: Hematuria, which is the presence of blood in the urine, can occur in hemophilia, but it is not the most common bleeding manifestation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Intracranial hemorrhage: Intracranial hemorrhage, which refers to bleeding within the skull or brain, is a severe complication of hemophilia but is relatively rare."}]},{"n":40,"q":"A patient with thalassemia has a history of multiple blood transfusions, iron overload, and cardiac arrhythmia. She has now come for blood transfusion and during the process, complains of backache and looks very anxious. What would you do next?","o":[{"t":"Stop the blood transfusion"},{"t":"Continue the transfusion but do an ECG"},{"t":"Stop the transfusion. Wait for patient to become normal and then start it again"},{"t":"Do clerical check and get ECG"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Stop the blood transfusion:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In a patient with thalassemia who has a history of multiple blood transfusions, iron overload, and cardiac arrhythmia, the presenting symptoms of backache and anxiety during a blood transfusion raise concerns for a transfusion reaction, specifically an acute hemolytic transfusion reaction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this scenario, the appropriate course of action is to stop the blood transfusion immediately to prevent further complications. Once the transfusion is stopped, the patient should be closely monitored for any signs of worsening symptoms, and supportive measures should be initiated as necessary."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Continue the transfusion but do an ECG: Continuing the transfusion without addressing the potential transfusion reaction can lead to further complications and risks the patient's safety."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Stop the transfusion, wait for the patient to become normal, and then start it again: This approach is not recommended because a suspected transfusion reaction requires prompt action. Stopping the transfusion is the first step to prevent the reaction from worsening and to ensure the patient's safety."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Do clerical check and get ECG: While a clerical check and ECG may be relevant in certain situations, such as ensuring the correct blood product is being transfused or assessing for potential cardiac complications."}]},{"n":41,"q":"What is incorrect regarding Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC)?","o":[{"t":"Increased schistocytes"},{"t":"Increased PT"},{"t":"Increased fibrinogen"},{"t":"Increased FDPs"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Increased fibrinogen:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased fibrinogen is wrong about DIC (Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"DIC is characterized by activation of coagulation system"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In DIC, fibrinogen levels decrease as it's consumed in clot formation, not increased, because it's used up in the excessive clotting process."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options :"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Increased schistocytes: In DIC, the presence of schistocytes is a characteristic finding due to the formation of microvascular clots and the subsequent shearing of red blood cells."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Increased PT:In DIC, there is activation of the coagulation system, leading to consumption of clotting factors. This consumption results in prolongation of PT, indicating impaired clotting function."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Increased FDPs: In DIC, there is increased activation of the fibrinolytic system, leading to increased levels of FDPs. Elevated levels of FDPs are often observed in DIC and can be used as a diagnostic marker."}]},{"n":42,"q":"For hairy cell leukemia, which drug is considered the preferred choice?","o":[{"t":"Rituximab"},{"t":"Vemurafenib"},{"t":"Cladribine"},{"t":"Interferon-alpha"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Cladribine:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cladribine (also known as 2-chlorodeoxyadenosine or 2-CdA) is a purine analog chemotherapy drug that has shown high efficacy in the treatment of hairy cell leukemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is considered the drug of choice for HCL because it achieves high response rates and durable remissions in most patients."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Deoxycoformycin is also shown to have a good remission rate."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Rituximab (incorrect): While rituximab has shown efficacy in the treatment of various B-cell lymphomas, it is not the drug of choice for hairy cell leukemia. Cladribine is the preferred treatment for HCL."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Vemurafenib (incorrect): Vemurafenib is a targeted therapy drug primarily used for the treatment of certain forms of metastatic melanoma with BRAF V600E mutation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Interferon-alpha (incorrect): Interferon-alpha is a type of immunotherapy that has been used in the treatment of hairy cell leukemia in the past. It is now less commonly used."}]},{"n":43,"q":"What is the next course of action for the management of a 65-year-old woman who arrived at the emergency department with a hemoglobin level of 5.4 g/dl, urea level of 86 mg/dl, creatinine level of 6 mg/dl, calcium level of 12, and potassium level of 4.4? Additionally, the bone marrow aspiration reveals over 60% plasma cells.","o":[{"t":"Urgent consultation for dialysis"},{"t":"Dexamethasone + lenalidomide"},{"t":"Dexamethasone + fluids"},{"t":"Bone marrow transplantation"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Dexamethasone + fluids:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this scenario, the patient's clinical presentation, laboratory findings, and bone marrow aspiration results suggest the possibility of multiple myeloma, a type of cancer involving plasma cells in the bone marrow."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The next line of management in this situation typically involves initiating treatment with a combination of dexamethasone (a corticosteroid) and fluids."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Urgent consultation for dialysis: Dialysis is not the immediate next step in the management of the presented case. Dialysis is primarily used in cases of severe renal failure where conservative management measures have failed."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Dexamethasone + lenalidomide: Lenalidomide (an immunomodulatory drug) is typically included in the treatment regimen for multiple myeloma, especially in combination with dexamethasone, but it is not the immediate next step. The initial management involves dexamethasone and fluids, and the addition of lenalidomide can be considered in subsequent treatment stages."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Bone marrow transplantation: Bone marrow transplantation is a treatment option for multiple myeloma in certain cases, particularly in younger patients or those with high-risk disease."}]},{"n":44,"q":"Mark the true statements 1. Pernicious anemia is an example of Type II Hypersensitivity 2. Serum Sickness is a example of Type III Hypersensitivity 3. Pathergy test is an example of Type IV Hypersensitivity 4. Pathergy test is done for Reiter's disease","o":[{"t":"1, 2 and 3 are true"},{"t":"2, 3 and 4 are true"},{"t":"1, 2, 3 and 4 are true"},{"t":"3 and 4 are true"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct option A - 1, 2 and 3 are true:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pernicious anemia is an autoimmune destruction of gastric parietal cells producing intrinsic factor, hindering B12 absorption. It is a Type II Hypersensitivity involves anti-intrinsic factor and anti-parietal cell antibodies targeting cell destruction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum Sickness is a type III Hypersensitivity reaction involving immune complex-mediated reactions to foreign serum or drugs forming immune complexes, causing tissue inflammation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pathergy test is a diagnostic tool for Behçet's disease, assessing abnormal skin reactions. It is a type IV Hypersensitivity mediated by T cells, and characterized by delayed skin responses."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The false statement is:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A Pathergy test is done for Reiter's disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The Pathergy test is not specifically performed for Reiter's disease but for the diagnosis of Behcet’s disease. Reiter's disease, also known as reactive arthritis, is characterized by joint inflammation, urethritis, and conjunctivitis. The diagnosis of Reiter's disease is based on clinical features."}]},{"n":45,"q":"Which of the following factors hinder the absorption of iron?","o":[{"t":"Vitamin C"},{"t":"Phytates"},{"t":"Oxalate"},{"t":"Myoglobin"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Phytates:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Phytates are naturally occurring compounds found in certain plant foods such as whole grains, legumes, and nuts . They have the ability to bind to iron and form insoluble complexes, which reduces the absorption of iron in the digestive tract . This can lead to decreased iron availability for the body."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Vitamin C: Vitamin C, also known as ascorbic acid, actually enhances iron absorption. It helps in the conversion of non-heme iron (the form of iron found in plant-based foods) to a more absorbable form."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Oxalate: Oxalate, found in certain foods like spinach, rhubarb, and beet greens, can affect calcium absorption, but they do not directly inhibit iron absorption."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Myoglobin: Myoglobin is a protein found in muscle tissue that stores oxygen. While myoglobin does contain iron, it is not involved in the absorption of iron from the diet."}]},{"n":46,"q":"In fever of unknown origin, blood sample should be drawn how many times?","o":[{"t":"2"},{"t":"3"},{"t":"4"},{"t":"5"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct option B - 3:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This option is correct. 3 sterile blood samples should be drawn and cultured. It is necessary to ensure ample growth time for any fastidious organisms, such as HACEK organisms and also to look for unusual organisms. But without potential diagnostic clues culturing of 3 blood samples will render no use."}]}]);
